“Probes in Kubernetes are health-check mechanisms used by kubelet to monitor container health and availability.

There are mainly three probes:

Liveness Probe
Used to check whether application is alive.
If it fails, Kubernetes restarts the container automatically.

Readiness Probe
Used to check whether application is ready to accept traffic.
If it fails, pod is removed from service endpoints but container is not restarted.

Startup Probe
Used for slow-starting applications to prevent premature restarts during startup phase.

Probes help achieve self-healing, high availability, and zero-downtime deployments in production Kubernetes environments.”

////////////
----------------------------------------------------------------
Important Difference
Probe	What Happens on Failure
Liveness	   Container restarted
Readiness	   Traffic stopped and evicted from endpoints
-----------------------------------------------------------------
3.Startup Probe
Used for slow-starting applications.

Example
startupProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /startup
    port: 8080

  failureThreshold: 30
  periodSeconds: 10

Meaning:
Application gets 300 seconds to start



Real Production Example
A common production setup:

Readiness Probe
→ controls traffic

Liveness Probe
→ handles stuck/crashed apps

Startup Probe
→ protects slow-starting apps

Simple Visualization
********************
Container Starts
       ↓
Startup Probe
       ↓
Readiness Probe
       ↓
Traffic Starts
       ↓
Liveness Probe monitors continuously


Types of Probe Checks
Kubernetes supports 3 probe methods:
Type          	Description
HTTP GET	      Calls HTTP endpoint
TCP Socket	    Checks port open or not
Exec Command	  Runs command inside container


